 closer  and closer and they begin to join  together and they lose their stability  the emulsion doesn't remain drops of one  fluid another but rather phase separates  becomes the two fluids again and if you  look at them if we watch them over time  you see they completely face that way so  this emulsion is not stable so how do we  make an emulsion an emulsion is mixture  of one fluid in a second by making drops  for example in this case we've made oil  drops in water the oil drops are now  called the dispersed phase the water is  called the continuous phase and to make  an emulsion you have to mix them  together somehow you have to form drops  of the dispersed phase in the continuous  phase and when you do that you get these  drops and that's your emulsion but to  make them stable you have to prevent the  drops you have to keep the drops as  individual drops you can't allow them to  join together or coalesce somehow you  have to stabilize the interface of the  drops of the oil in the water and we do  that using a surfactant a surfactant is  a surface active molecule it's an amp of  silica molecule part of it is water like  a hydrophilic and the other part is oil  like or how  phobic and they're joined together so  the surfactant molecules like both the  oil and the water and it can't really  decide which they want so they can't  really sit in either the oil or the  water instead they prefer to sit at the  interface at the surface between the two  fluids typically you put the surfactant  in the continuous phase so if you have  an oil and water emulsion you put the  surfactant in the water so part of the  surfactant sits at the oil there's  excess surfactant however you need to  have access to factions so that you  always have more surfactants available  so that it can come to the interface as  you create the interface when you're  emulsifying something the surfactant has  to come out of the water and go to the  interface however the surfactant does  not like to stay in the water because it  has part of it which is oil like it  doesn't like to be in water so instead  the surfactant forms aggregates we call  these micelles the aggregate so that the  tail groups the oil part of the  surfactants are all connected together  they're all facing one another excluding  some of the water that allow the  surfactant to exist in the water without  exposing the tail groups to the water  itself but the micelles act as a  reservoir they can easily break up and  bring new surfactant to the interface as  you create the interface and if you have  enough surfactant and egg yolk for  example is an excellent surfactant they  completely coat all the interfaces and  then when drops come together as I show  here in this little cartoon when two  drops come together with a coated  interface with a surfactant covered  interface it prevents the drops from  joined together and becoming larger  drops so the drops remain stable and the  emulsion remains stable
 

